  ok\ explicitly redistributable per its own copyright notice.
  ok\ ============================================================
  ok\ ---- section-marker: makes this file independently runnable ----
  ok\ M24 snapshots the dictionary/HERE state right before this
  ok\ file's own definitions begin. Invoking M24 at the end (below)
  ok\ erases everything this file defined - including M24 itself -
  ok\ restoring the system to the state it was in just after
  ok\ ttester.fs + 00_test_prelude.fs were loaded. This lets the
  ok\ automation load/run/reset one section file at a time, in any
  ok\ order, without cross-file dictionary pollution.
  okMARKER M24
  ok\ Reset the pass/fail counters, record the stack depth.
  okTEST-BEGIN
  ok\ F.6.1.0560  >IN
  okVARIABLE SCANS
  ok\ This test must retain the multiline split 
  ok\ to proceed to completion by scanning multiple lines.
  ok\ It infinite loops in INTERPRET otherwise.
  ok: RESCAN? -1 SCANS +! SCANS @ IF 0 >IN ! THEN ;
  okT{ 2 SCANS !
  ok 345 RESCAN?
  ok -> 345 345 }T  Actual:Expected 345 =?= 345 345 =?= 345 
  ok: GS2 5 SCANS ! S" 123 RESCAN?" EVALUATE ;
  okT{ GS2 -> 123 123 123 123 123 }T  Actual:Expected 123 =?= 123 123 =?= 123 123 =?= 123 123 =?= 123 123 =?= 123 
  ok\ These tests must start on a new line
  ok\ This block's literals (123456, 14145, 8115...) need DECIMAL, but
  ok\ the >NUMBER section right below needs ambient HEX back - its own
  ok\ expected results include bare hex digits ("F", and BL's "20")
  ok\ that aren't valid decimal numbers at all. Previously this file
  ok\ called DECIMAL here and never switched back, which broke its own
  ok\ later >NUMBER tests (not just the next file in load order, as
  ok\ with 08/11/12 - this one broke itself). Save/restore locally.
  ok\ BUG FIX (round 1): was "BASE @ >R DECIMAL ... R> BASE !" - unsafe
  ok\ spanning separate top-level lines (see 08_defining_words.tests.fs's
  ok\ matching comment for the full >R/R> mechanism). (GN1 and
  ok\ >NUMBER-BASED below use >R/R> too, but entirely inside their own
  ok\ single colon-definition, executed as one unbroken call - that's
  ok\ the safe, standard use and is left alone.)
  ok\ BUG FIX (round 2): plain "BASE @ DECIMAL ... BASE !" is also
  ok\ fragile - an uncaught error anywhere in between wipes a parked
  ok\ stack value via QLOOP's top-level recovery. Unconditional restore
  ok\ instead - see 08_defining_words.tests.fs for the full reasoning.
  ok\ BUG FIX (cell size): the original Annex F text used 123456 here,
  ok\ which needs a 32-bit cell - in a 16-bit cell it wraps to $E240
  ok\ (negative as signed), so "9 <" is wrongly true on the very first
  ok\ pass, >IN lands on the "IN" of ">IN" and BADWORD throws -13. The
  ok\ standard itself later changed this test to 12345 for 16-bit
  ok\ systems (forth-standard.org >IN page). Expected results below
  ok\ follow from the same digit-stripping: 12345 2345 345 45 5.
  ok\ BUG FIX (missing text): the second test originally ended
  ok\ "14 >IN ! GCD calculation" - those two words are deliberately
  ok\ skipped over by the ">IN +!" 34-character jump; without them the
  ok\ jump overshoots the end of the line instead of landing on "->".
  ok\ Both tests rely on >IN pointing at or beyond the end of the line
  ok\ being treated as end-of-input (WORD/PARSE/PARSE-NAME fix).
  ok\ Kept in the standard's own two-line layout, which deliberately
  ok\ exercises >IN landing just past the end of the first line.
  okDECIMAL
  okT{ 12345 DEPTH OVER 9 < 35 AND + 3 + >IN !
  ok-> 12345 2345 345 45 5 }T  Actual:Expected 5 =?= 5 45 =?= 45 345 =?= 345 2345 =?= 2345 12345 =?= 12345 
  okT{ 14145 8115 ?DUP 0= 34 AND >IN +! TUCK MOD 14 >IN ! GCD calculation
  ok-> 15 }T  Actual:Expected 15 =?= 15 
  okHEX
  ok\ F.6.1.0570  >NUMBER
